# PART 6 — MESSAGE PROTOCOL

Do not send arbitrary JSON strings without considering the fact that Bluetooth socket streams do not automatically preserve application-level message boundaries.

Design a well-defined protocol.

## 6.1 Message Envelope

Use a documented message envelope containing appropriate fields such as:

- Protocol version
- Message type
- Message ID
- Conversation ID
- Sender device ID
- Recipient device ID
- Timestamp
- Payload length
- Payload
- Optional integrity or authentication metadata

Choose an encoding format appropriate for the project.

Length-prefixed framing is an acceptable approach.

Validate lengths before allocating memory.

Reject malformed messages.

Set reasonable maximum payload sizes.

Do not trust remote device input.

## 6.2 Message Types

Support the protocol messages needed for:

- Connection handshake
- Device verification
- Text message
- Message acknowledgement
- Delivery receipt
- Error response
- Ping or connection health check, if needed
- Attachment metadata
- Attachment data chunks
- Transfer completion
- Transfer cancellation

Implement only the message types required by the current development phase.

Do not expose unsupported protocol features in the UI.

## 6.3 Protocol Compatibility

Include a protocol version.

Reject unsupported versions gracefully or negotiate compatible capabilities where appropriate.

Document changes to the protocol.

Do not make a protocol change that silently breaks existing installations.

