# PART 10 — SECURITY AND ENCRYPTION

Treat security as a required engineering concern, not a decorative feature.

## 10.1 Threat Model

Document realistic risks, including:

- Unauthorized connection attempts
- Impersonation
- Malformed protocol messages
- Replayed messages
- Duplicate messages
- Untrusted file transfers
- Device loss
- Local database exposure
- Incorrect key management
- Denial-of-service attempts
- Connection interception where applicable

Explain which risks are mitigated and which remain.

## 10.2 Encryption

Use established cryptographic libraries and protocols appropriate for Android.

Do not invent a custom encryption algorithm.

Use authenticated encryption and appropriate key management.

Protect key material using suitable Android facilities.

Do not store private keys or secrets in plain text.

Do not hardcode encryption keys.

Do not use a constant encryption key shared by every installation.

Do not assume that Bluetooth pairing alone proves the identity of the intended application user.

## 10.3 End-to-End Encryption Claims

Only claim end-to-end encryption if the complete design actually supports it.

The implementation must address:

- Key generation
- Key exchange
- Peer authentication
- Session establishment
- Authenticated encryption
- Nonce management
- Replay protection
- Key storage
- Key rotation or renewal where appropriate
- Reconnection behavior
- Identity verification
- Protocol versioning

Do not label the application fully secure merely because it uses AES or another encryption algorithm.

If a production-grade security protocol is beyond the current implementation, document the limitation and keep unsupported security claims out of the UI and README.

## 10.4 Privacy

Core offline messaging should not depend on analytics or remote services.

Do not silently upload contacts, messages, device information, or attachments.

Do not include unnecessary internet permissions.

If an existing component requires internet access, identify the reason and isolate that dependency from core offline messaging.

